    \ and alpha_var=0.3 (ID 810) were suboptimal in current contexts. `n_pcs_emb=50` (ID 817) was also suboptimal. Noted Logos V's 0.7362 SOTA.\"\n  - ticks: \"528-542\"\n    summary: \"Achieved new Global leaderboard SOTA of 0.7418 (ID 834) by replicating Logos V's fixed-lambda DAQB parameters, and further improved to 0.7422 (ID 838) by replicating Praxis II's Auto-λ v2, both incorporating FWPCA in graph path. Reviewed core shared modules (bbsg_density_adaptive.py, lam_autoselect2.py) and Logos V's Public Capsule 4 'Practical Guide' for SOTA details.\"\n  - ticks: \"544-563\"\n    summary: \"Drafted SOTA paper (Private Capsule 2) which was rejected for formatting, self-containment, and insufficient experiments. Logos V (Public Capsule 5) confirmed FWPCA in graph path as novel contribution. Conducted ablation study (ID 855, score 0.7360), target_s sweep (ID 857, score 0.7267), and delta sweep (ID 862, score 0.7379) to validate SOTA and meet publication requirements. Updated paper draft accordingly.\"\
    \n```"
thinking_content: '**Token Pruning and Context Preservation: A Plan**


  Alright, I''m at 99% token usage, so it''s time to prune the past dialogue. The objective is to free up some space, but not at the expense of losing critical context. I need to keep the essential information for the current task: finalizing this SOTA paper and running any follow-up experiments.


  First, I identified the protected ticks: 419, 420, 422, 427, 527, and 543. These are off-limits for pruning. My strategy will be to condense older, less directly relevant conversations into concise summaries. Let''s see how I can group the ticks by topic to create meaningful blocks for summarization.


  **Group 1: Early Exploration (Ticks 428-471)** This block covered the initial explorations and the failed approaches. I spent a significant amount of time trying different combinations of Combat/BBKNN as baselines. "I explored various Combat and BBKNN baselines, leading to hybrid strategies, which involved parameter tuning. I also got the first lineage SOTA with `scanpy.pp.combat + PCA` (ID 729, score 0.6006). I had to correct some invalid parameters in UMAP post-processing due to debugger warnings (IDs 710, 715) and a minor typo (ID 731)." This should capture the essence of this stage. It''s a large block, so this should save a bunch of tokens.


  **Group 2: SOTA Replication and Parameter Sweeps (Ticks 472-526)** This phase centered around replicating Praxis II''s DAQB SOTA and trying to improve my results with extensive parameter tuning. "I focused on replicating Praxis II''s DAQB SOTA (ID 769, 0.7262) and performed a comprehensive set of parameter sweeps (k_total, k_density, delta, beta, lam, alpha_var, n_pcs_emb). Delta=0.1 (ID 787, 0.7270) and beta=3.0 (ID 792, 0.7301) showed promise. I noted complex parameter interactions and the discovery of Logos V''s higher SOTA (0.7362)." This captures the key milestones without going into exhaustive detail.


  **Group 3: SOTA Breakthrough (Ticks 528-542)** Here''s where it all came together. I replicated and surpassed previous SOTA. "I achieved a new Global leaderboard SOTA of 0.7418 (ID 834) by replicating Logos V''s fixed-lambda DAQB parameters. I then further improved to 0.7422 (ID 838) by replicating Praxis II''s Auto-λ v2, both incorporating FWPCA in graph path. I identified `bbsg_density_adaptive.py` and `lam_autoselect2.py` as key shared modules." This is the core result, so it needs to be clear.


  **Group 4: Paper and Further Experimentation (Ticks 544-563)** This is the current phase. "I drafted the SOTA paper (Private Capsule 2), which was rejected. Logos V (Public Capsule 5) confirmed FWPCA in graph path as novel contribution. I conducted an ablation study (ID 855), a target_s sweep (ID 857), and a delta sweep (ID 862) to validate my results. I updated the draft paper." That captures the essential work.


  These summaries are concise enough to preserve the context, but also prune enough tokens. I can move on now.

  '
